<div class="ind-flow ind-section ind-section--inset:start-collapse" data-component-id="ind2:section">
      
                                                          
          



<div data-component-id="ind2:meta" class="ind-meta">
      <div class="ind-meta__item">
                      <span class="ind-meta__label">Last update: </span>
      
                  <time datetime="2025-08-12T12:00:00Z">12 August 2025</time>

          

          </div>
  </div>

              
      


  <p class="ind-paragraph--lead">You want to apply for a residence permit and need a provisional residence permit (in Dutch: <em><span lang="nl" dir="ltr">machtiging tot voorlopig verblijf</span></em> or MVV ). You must apply for the MVV and residence permit at the same time. You apply at the Dutch embassy or consulate abroad. Find out how to submit the application and which application form you need.</p>


      
      
          
      </div>

  

                        
  






<section class="ind-flow ind-section ind-section--inset:start-collapse" data-component-id="ind2:section">
      
                              




<h2 id="make-an-appointment-at-the-dutch-embassy-or-consulate" data-component-id="ind2:heading" class="ind-heading ind-heading--h2">
      Make an appointment at the Dutch embassy or consulate
  </h2>

  
  

  
  

  <p>Before you can apply for <a href="/en/provisional-residence-permit-mvv" data-entity-type="node" data-entity-uuid="92441f75-463f-4e02-89cc-51a010a86f2a" data-entity-substitution="canonical" title="Provisional residence permit (MVV)">the MVV</a> and residence permit, you must make an appointment at the Dutch embassy or consulate&nbsp;in your country of origin, or country of continuous residence.</p><p>Your country of origin is the country of which you have the nationality. The country of continuous residence is the country where you may stay for more than 3 months. Because you have a residence permit or are waiting for a decision on a residence application.</p><p><span>Go to netherlandsworldwide.nl for information on </span><a href="https://www.netherlandsworldwide.nl/making-appointment" title="how to make an appointment at a Dutch embassy or consulate"><span>how to make an appointment at a Dutch embassy or consulate</span></a><span>.</span></p><p>If there is no Dutch embassy or consulate in your country of origin or country of continuous residence, you make an appointment at the nearest Dutch embassy or consulate.</p>

<div class="ind:clear-both"></div>




          
      </section>


  






<section class="ind-flow ind-section" data-component-id="ind2:section">
      
                              




<h2 id="information-about-your-appointment" data-component-id="ind2:heading" class="ind-heading ind-heading--h2">
      Information about your appointment
  </h2>

  
  

  
  

  <p>Take the completed application form, your passport photo and your valid passport with you to the appointment. Sometimes you need to bring extra documents. These are listed in the application form. A staff member of the Dutch embassy or consulate will register your application and fill in the application date on the application form. The staff member will also fill in the details of the Dutch embassy or consulate. You will get the form back afterwards. You will receive a confirmation of receipt with your personal V-number.</p><p>The Dutch embassy or consulate will also take your biometric data: you have to provide your signature and fingerprints. These data are needed for the residence permit.</p><h3>Minor children</h3><p>Children under 12 must go to the Dutch embassy or consulate, together with a legal representative. A legal representative is a parent with parental authority or a guardian.</p>

<div class="ind:clear-both"></div>




          
      </section>


  






<section class="ind-flow ind-section" data-component-id="ind2:section">
      
                              




<h2 id="send-application-to-ind" data-component-id="ind2:heading" class="ind-heading ind-heading--h2">
      Send application to IND
  </h2>

  
  

  
  

  <p>Have you completely filled in the application form? And have you collected all requested documents? Then send your own application with the documents to the IND in the Netherlands. You must do this within 3 weeks of the date that the Dutch embassy or consulate filled in on your application form. On the form you can find out where you must send the application.</p>

<div class="ind:clear-both"></div>




          
      </section>


  






<section class="ind-flow ind-section" data-component-id="ind2:section">
      
                              




<h2 id="pay-costs-of-application" data-component-id="ind2:heading" class="ind-heading ind-heading--h2">
      Pay costs of application
  </h2>

  
  

  
  

  <p>You must <a href="/en/fees-costs-of-an-application" title="costs" data-entity-type="node" data-entity-uuid="dd3911c6-a598-4819-95eb-96fbd0d2507b" data-entity-substitution="canonical">pay a fee</a> to be able to apply. You must do so within 3 weeks of the date that the Dutch embassy or consulate filled in on your application form. The application form explains how you must pay the costs.</p>

<div class="ind:clear-both"></div>




          
      </section>


  






<section class="ind-flow ind-section" data-component-id="ind2:section">
      
                              




<h2 id="application-form" data-component-id="ind2:heading" class="ind-heading ind-heading--h2">
      Application form
  </h2>

  
  

  
  

  <p>Is your residence purpose not listed in one of the forms below? Then your sponsor (recognised sponsor) probably has to apply to the IND in the Netherlands. For more information, go to the web page about <a href="/en/residence-permits" title="Residence permits" data-entity-type="node" data-entity-uuid="75bda1cb-1af2-4106-a469-4382b391f50f" data-entity-substitution="canonical">your residence permit</a>.</p><h3>Family: (married) partner and minor child</h3><p>  



      </p><figure>
  
  
  
  


